Targeted drugs for multiple sclerosis (MS) are specialized therapies designed to modify disease activity by acting on specific immune pathways associated with inflammation and neurological damage. Treatment options include monoclonal antibodies, sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators, and other disease-modifying therapies. These medicines aim to reduce relapse frequency, limit new disease activity, and help manage the long-term progression of MS.

The market is evolving as pharmaceutical companies develop therapies with improved efficacy, safety profiles, and administration options. Injectable treatments remain important for patients who require closely monitored therapy, while oral medications offer convenience for suitable patients. Research into B-cell depletion, Bruton’s tyrosine kinase (BTK) inhibitors, remyelination, and neuroprotection is also creating new avenues for treatment development.

