Advances in Disease-Modifying Therapies
Innovation in biologics, monoclonal antibodies, and targeted immunomodulators is reshaping treatment options. Therapies directed at specific immune pathways can provide alternatives for patients who do not respond adequately to existing treatments or who require a different treatment approach.

Research into B-cell depletion and S1P receptor modulation continues to influence the therapeutic landscape. BTK inhibitors and other investigational approaches are also attracting attention for their potential to address different aspects of MS disease activity and progression.

Growing Preference for Convenient Administration
Oral disease-modifying therapies offer an alternative to injectable and infusion-based treatments for eligible patients. Convenience, treatment preferences, and the demands of long-term disease management can influence therapy selection.

The report identifies growing interest in oral formulations, while injectable treatments remain important for patients who need specific therapies and clinical monitoring. Pharmaceutical companies are therefore pursuing improvements in efficacy, tolerability, and administration to meet varied patient needs.

